<h3 id=3D"toc-hId-1656016608"><br>Retrieve and Set Macro Variables</h3>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>Users can retrieve and set macro variables within PROC R with the <span>=
symget()</span> and <span>symput()</span> functions.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><strong>symget():</strong> retrieves the value of a SAS macro variable.<=
/p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span>symget("Make")</span> # Retrieves the value of the Make macro vari=
able</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><strong>symput():</strong> sets the value of a SAS macro variable.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span>symput("Make", "Audi")</span> # Sets the value of Make to Audi</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p data-start=3D"68" data-end=3D"356">This example shows how <span>symget()=
</span>and <span>symput()</span>enable two-way communication between SAS an=
d R inside PROC R. The SAS macro variable Make is passed into R using <span=
>symget()</span>, where it is used to filter the SASHELP.CARS data set and =
calculate the average of city and highway MPG. That result is then sent bac=
k to SAS using <span>symput()</span>, creating a new macro variable (AvgMPG=
) that is used directly in the SAS report title.</p>
<p data-start=3D"68" data-end=3D"356">&nbsp;</p>
<pre class=3D"  language-sas"><code class=3D"  language-sas"><span class=3D=
"token macro-keyword keyword">%let</span> Make <span class=3D"token operato=
r">=3D</span> Honda<span class=3D"token punctuation">;</span>

<span class=3D"token comment">/* R: filter data and create a macro variable=
 */</span>
